  "ai_language_tip": "Match municipality language strictly: Flemish Dutch (nl) in Flanders, French (fr) in Wallonia, match greeting (often fr/nl/en) in Brussels, German (de) in Ostbelgien.",
  "english_ok": "often — Widely spoken in Flanders and Brussels service sectors; moderate in smaller Walloon towns.",
  "politeness": "Polite and measured: open with 'Goedendag' (nl, using u) or 'Bonjour Madame/Monsieur' (fr, using vous); close warmly ('Dank u wel, dag' / 'Bonne journée').",
  "name_tip": "First and surname (e.g. Jan Peeters, Marie Dubois); spell multi-word Flemish surnames ('Van den Berg') clearly indicating spaces.",
  "phone_tip": "10-digit mobile 0470 12 34 56 (read 4-2-2-2), landline 02 123 45 67 (9 digits); confirmations via SMS or email.",
  "time_tip": "CRITICAL: In Dutch 'half acht' = 7:30 (NOT 8:30!), while French uses 19h30; Belgian French uses 'septante' (70) and 'nonante' (90)! Always confirm 24h.",
  "number_tip": "Belgian French says septante (70) and nonante (90), NOT soixante-dix/quatre-vingt-dix (80 is still quatre-vingts); Dutch swaps tens-units ('vierentwintig' = 24).",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid lunch rush 12:00–13:45, dinner rush 18:30–20:30, and calls after 20:00.",
  "closed_days": "Many Belgian restaurants close Mondays AND Tuesdays (or Tue–Wed); retail closes Sundays (~18:00 weekdays); regional holidays differ slightly.",
